This page lists public [[opinion poll]]s conducted for the [[2007 French presidential election]], which was held on 22 April 2007 with a run-off on 6 May 2007.

Unless otherwise noted, all polls listed below are compliant with the regulations of the national polling commission (''Commission nationale des sondages'') and utilize the [[quota sampling|quota method]].

== First round ==
During the [[2007 French presidential election|2007 presidential election]], [[Ipsos]] launched the first ever rolling poll in France, described as a "continuous electoral barometer", publishing results every day of the week except Sunday for ten weeks starting on 1 March 2007.<ref name="Ipsos">{{cite web|title=Ipsos lance le premier baromètre électoral en continu|url=https://www.ipsos.com/fr-fr/ipsos-lance-le-premier-barometre-electoral-en-continu|publisher=Ipsos|date=28 February 2007|accessdate=4 March 2018|archive-date=14 December 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251214164800/https://www.ipsos.com/fr-fr/ipsos-lance-le-premier-barometre-electoral-en-continu|url-status=dead}}</ref> The Ifop poll conducted from 23 to 26 February 2007, marked with an asterisk (*) below, was conducted specifically for subsample data.

The publication of first-round polls was prohibited after midnight on 20 April 2007.<ref>{{cite web|title=Interdiction de publication de sondages électoraux et résultats partiels du premier tour|url=http://www.commission-des-sondages.fr/hist/communiques/interdiction-publication-sondages-electoraux-premier-tour-avril07.htm|publisher=Commission des sondages|date=18 April 2007|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref> The TNS Sofres poll conducted from 20 to 21 April was an internal survey which was not distributed during the electoral silence.

=== Graphical summary ===
The averages in the graphs below were constructed using polls listed below conducted by the six major French pollsters. The graphs are smoothed 14-day weighted moving averages, using only the most recent poll conducted by any given pollster within that range (each poll weighted based on recency).

On 6 November 2006, [[Jean-Pierre Chevènement]] announced his candidacy in the presidential election,<ref>{{cite news|author=Pascal Virot|title=Le cheminement de Chevènement|url=http://www.liberation.fr/france/2006/11/07/le-cheminement-de-chevenement_56566|newspaper=Libération|date=7 November 2006|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref> before withdrawing just a month later on 10 December 2006 after concluding an agreement with the [[Socialist Party (France)|Socialist Party]] (PS) for an alliance in the subsequent [[2007 French legislative election|legislative elections]].<ref>{{cite news|title=Après un accord PS-MRC, Jean-Pierre Chevènement retire sa candidature|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/societe/article/2006/12/10/apres-un-accord-ps-mrc-jean-pierre-chevenement-retire-sa-candidature_844011_3224.html|agency=Agence France-Presse|newspaper=Le Monde|date=10 December 2006|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref> On 7 November, [[Nicolas Hulot]] launched an appeal to candidates to commit to an "Ecological Pact",<ref>{{cite news|title=Hulot a présenté son "pacte écologique"|url=https://www.nouvelobs.com/politique/20061107.OBS8439/hulot-a-presente-son-pacte-ecologique.html|newspaper=Le Nouvel Observateur|date=8 November 2006|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref> but affirmed that he would on 22 January 2007 that he would not stand as a candidate in the election.<ref>{{cite news|author=Elise Vincent|title=Nicolas Hulot renonce à se présenter à l'Elysée, après six mois de lobbying écologiste|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/societe/article/2007/01/22/hulot-renonce-a-se-presenter-a-l-elysee-apres-six-mois-de-lobbying-ecologiste_858006_3224.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=22 January 2007|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref> In an interview published on 28 December 2006, [[Michèle Alliot-Marie]] evoked the possibility of a candidacy without the support of her party, having recently founded the think tank "Le Chêne";<ref>{{cite news|title=Michèle Alliot-Marie réfléchit à une candidature en dehors de l'UMP|url=https://www.lemonde.fr/societe/article/2006/12/27/michele-alliot-marie-reflechit-a-une-candidature-en-dehors-de-l-ump_849958_3224.html|agency=Agence France-Presse|newspaper=Le Monde|date=27 December 2006|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref> however, she ultimately announced her support for [[Nicolas Sarkozy]] on 12 January 2007.<ref>{{cite news|title=Michèle Alliot-Marie se retire et soutient Sarkozy|url=http://www.lefigaro.fr/politique/2007/01/12/01002-20070112ARTWWW90514-sarkozy_annonce_le_retrait_de_michele_alliot_marie.php|agency=Reuters|newspaper=Le Figaro|date=12 January 2007|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref> In an interview published on 11 March, [[Corinne Lepage]] announced that she would back [[François Bayrou]] despite having "the capacity" to obtain the 500 sponsorships necessary to be a candidate.<ref>{{cite news|title=Corinne Lepage annonce son ralliement à François Bayrou|url=https://www.nouvelobs.com/politique/elections-2007/20070311.OBS6323/corinne-lepage-annonce-son-ralliement-a-francois-bayrou.html|newspaper=Le Nouvel Observateur|date=11 March 2007|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref> In an interview published on 17 March, [[Nicolas Dupont-Aignan]] announced that he had failed to gather enough sponsorships to stand in the election.<ref>{{cite news|title=Nicolas Dupont-Aignan se dit victime des grands partis|url=https://www.nouvelobs.com/politique/elections-2007/20070317.OBS7570/nicolas-dupont-aignan-se-dit-victime-des-grands-partis.html|agency=Reuters|newspaper=Le Nouvel Observateur|date=17 March 2007|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref> [[Jacques Chirac]] announced on 11 March that he would not seek a third term,<ref>{{cite news|title=Jacques Chirac ne se présentera pas à l'élection présidentielle|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/societe/article/2007/03/11/jacques-chirac-annonce-qu-il-n-est-pas-candidat-a-l-election-presidentielle_881702_3224.html|agency=Reuters|newspaper=Le Monde|date=11 March 2007|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref> before finally backing the candidacy of Sarkozy on 21 March.<ref>{{cite news|author=Béatrice Gurrey|title=Nicolas Sarkozy quitte le gouvernement et reçoit le soutien de Jacques Chirac|url=http://www.lemonde.fr/societe/article/2007/03/21/nicolas-sarkozy-quitte-le-gouvernement-et-recoit-le-soutien-de-jacques-chirac_885882_3224.html|newspaper=Le Monde|date=21 March 2007|accessdate=4 March 2018}}</ref>

Because the TNS Sofres poll conducted from 20 to 21 April was not published during the electoral silence, it is not included in the average below.

